I see - `copy_nword` copies the word into `token[STRINGMAX]` (1024 bytes), truncating at 1023 chars. So `cptr` after `copy_nword` points to the rest of the line (the remaining characters after the first word). 

Wait, let me re-read the code flow:

```c
cptr = copy_nword(cptr, token, sizeof(token));
```

`copy_nword` copies the first word (up to `len` bytes) into `token` and returns a pointer to the rest of the string. So after this call:
- `token` = "includefile" (the first word)
- `cptr` = pointer to the rest of the line, which is the long path

So `cptr` points into the `line` buffer which is dynamically allocated and can be very long. The path itself is not truncated by `copy_nword` - only the token is.

